---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from "../lib/schema";

const description =
  "On Marc Augé's concept of the 'non-place' — airports, malls and hotel rooms — and why a space only becomes a place once people inhabit it, leave their traces, and give it memory.";

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Non-Places", path: "/non-places" },
  ]),
  articleSchema({
    headline: "Non-Places",
    description,
    path: "/non-places",
  }),
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Non-Places" description={description} path="/non-places" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-10 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <a href="/about" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-stone hover:text-ink">
        ← About
      </a>
      <p class="coord-mark mt-6">Notes on place</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-5xl">Non-Places</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink/75">{description}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 pb-16 md:px-10 md:pb-24">
    <div class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-3xl">
      <img
        src="/images/non-places/steve-bishop.jpg"
        alt="Hazy, purple-lit corridor installation with translucent walls and waiting-room chairs"
        class="h-auto w-full"
      />
      <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs text-stone">
        Steve Bishop, <em>Deliquescing</em> (installation view at KW, Berlin), 2018. Photo by
        Frank Sperling. Courtesy the artist, Carlos/Ishikawa and KW Institute for Contemporary
        Art.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p>
        Much of modern life now takes place in spaces that do not belong to any specific
        location. Airports, shopping malls, highways, hotels, metro stations, waiting lounges,
        and chain stores are the most prominent examples of these. The French anthropologist
        Marc Augé defines such areas as "non-places." This term does not describe a space that
        is physically non-existent; rather, it refers to spaces that fail to establish a strong
        connection with people, memory, and identity.
      </p>
      <p>
        We can better understand this distinction if we consider that a place consists of more
        than just walls, a floor, and a roof. People imbue the spaces they inhabit with
        memories, stories, habits, and meanings. A neighborhood coffeehouse, a street where one
        spent their childhood, the kitchen of a family home, or a workshop used for many years is
        not merely a physical environment. In these spaces, the past and the present are linked.
        Individuals can situate themselves there and form connections with other people, events,
        and their own pasts. Thus, the space transforms into a vessel for memory.
      </p>
      <p>
        A "non-place," conversely, often represents the opposite of this relationship. A person
        at an airport is there not to live, but to travel elsewhere. A traveler on a highway
        moves through the environment without engaging with it. A hotel room is used for a few
        hours or days before being handed over to the next occupant. Shopping malls replicate
        similar stores, signage, lighting, and circulation patterns across cities worldwide. In
        these spaces, the individual becomes a transient user of a system rather than someone
        belonging to a specific place.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this reason, the concept of the "non-place" is closely linked to the idea of a lack
        of memory. However, it is impossible to speak of a space that is truly devoid of memory.
        People generate experiences in every environment they inhabit, and these experiences can
        become part of their personal memory. A farewell at an airport can transform into an
        incredibly powerful memory years later. Consequently, what often lacks memory is not the
        individual's experience, but the narrative structure of the space itself. The space does
        not reveal its past to the user; it renders the traces of previous users invisible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Viewed from this perspective, the "non-place" represents the constantly renewing face of
        the modern world. Signs, screens, wayfinding systems, security measures, and a
        standardized design language dictate what the user must do: "pass through here," "wait
        here," "scan your card," "proceed to the gate." Instead of forging a long-term
        relationship with the space, the individual moves according to instructions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps the real issue today is not that spaces physically resemble one another. What
        matters more is that the relationship people form with spaces is becoming increasingly
        transient. A place acquires meaning only when it establishes a connection with the past,
        with people, and with experiences. When this bond begins to fray, spaces in different
        parts of the world become interchangeable. Thus, a person may be present in a place
        without truly belonging to it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl border-t border-stone-light/70 pt-10">
      <p class="coord-mark">Where this leads</p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-ink/75">
        It's why {SITE.name} designs spaces open to change, use, and transformation — so a
        building can become a place, not a non-place, once the people who use it leave their
        traces on it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
        <a
          href="/about"
          class="inline-block bg-ink px-6 py-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-paper hover:bg-patina"
        >
          Read our approach
        </a>
        <a
          href="/design-with-memory"
          class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-patina hover:underline"
        >
          Continue reading: Design with Memory →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
